# Committed on purpose: NAMES and shapes, never values. Copy to `.env` (ignored) and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT
#
# It is not the application's configuration, and there is deliberately no production `.env`.
# Nothing in this project loads a dotenv file — the app reads `application.properties`, then
# `application-{profile}.properties`, then real environment variables and `--args` (AGENTS.md,
# "Configuration"). A `.env` here is a convenience for YOUR SHELL when running tools and scripts by
# hand; the process still receives ordinary environment variables, exactly as it would in production.
#
# In production those variables come from the platform's secret store — Compose/Kubernetes env,
# GitHub Actions secrets for CI — not from a file in the repository. A committed production `.env`
# would put secrets in a PUBLIC git history, and `FailClosedGuard` already refuses to boot when
# full-shaped config appears under `standalone`, so the profile boundary is enforced by the app
# rather than by which file was sourced.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Developer tooling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SonarCloud. Kept in its own `.env.sonar` in practice so it can be sourced alone; host, organization
# and projectKey are in pom.xml because only the token is secret.
SONAR_TOKEN=

# OWASP Dependency-Check (spec §12.1 stage 11). Kept in its own `.env.nvd` in practice, like
# SONAR_TOKEN above, so it can be sourced alone. Request one at
# https://nvd.nist.gov/developers/request-an-api-key — it is free and arrives by email.
#
# It is NOT optional for speed: without a key the NVD feed is rate-limited so hard that the first
# update takes tens of minutes or fails outright. CI treats it the way it treats SONAR_TOKEN —
# absent means the step SKIPS and says so loudly, never that it passed — so a fork's build stays
# green without one. Present means the scan runs and `failBuildOnCVSS` can fail the build.
NVD_API_KEY=

# ── Running the e2e suite by hand (scripts/e2e/run-e2e.sh) ───────────────────────────────────────
# These mirror the `Stage 9` step in .github/workflows/ci.yml. Without them the CLI defaults to the
# `standalone` profile and sends no token, so every scenario fails 401 against a `full` app — which
# is exactly what happened the first time this suite was run by hand.
LEDGER_PROFILE=full
#
# LEDGER_BASE_URL IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and removing it was a fix rather than a tidy-up. Since TLS
# landed the URL depends on E2E_MODE -- https://127.0.0.1:8443 through Traefik for the image,
# http://127.0.0.1:8080 straight to the process for the jar -- and run-e2e.sh exports whichever it
# chose. A value here would WIN over that (the script honours an explicit override), so a sourced
# .env would point the HTTPS run at a port nothing publishes and every scenario would fail to
# connect. Set it only if you are deliberately aiming the CLI somewhere else.
LEDGER_ISSUER_URI=http://localhost:8081/realms/tiny-ledger
LEDGER_CLIENT_ID=ledger-test
LEDGER_USERNAME=alice
# Committed test-fixture credential from docker/keycloak/realm-tiny-ledger.json, which is marked
# "never deploy". Written here as a value rather than a blank because it is already public and
# pretending otherwise would imply it is a secret.
LEDGER_PASSWORD=dev-only

# ── Compose ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5432 is the most contested port on a developer machine. If Postgres fails to start with
# "port is already allocated", set this to something free — docker-compose.yml and run-e2e.sh both
# read it, and the app is pointed at the same port on the command line.
TINY_LEDGER_PG_PORT=5432

# Traefik's published ports (spec §6.4a). 8443 is HTTPS and is the ONLY way into the application;
# 8000 is plaintext and answers a 301 to it. The app itself publishes nothing. Same escape hatch as
# TINY_LEDGER_PG_PORT above, for the same reason.
TINY_LEDGER_HTTPS_PORT=8443
TINY_LEDGER_HTTP_PORT=8000

# The Compose network, and Traefik's fixed address on it. THESE TWO MOVE TOGETHER — the address must
# be inside the subnet, and the application trusts `X-Forwarded-For` from that address and nothing
# else (§6.1), so changing one without the other either breaks routing or, worse, quietly widens the
# trust.
#
# Set them only on a collision. The first value chosen was already held by an unrelated project on
# this machine and Compose refused with
#   invalid pool request: Pool overlaps with other one on this address space
# The `.250` is high on purpose: Docker allocates dynamic addresses from the bottom of the range, so
# a low static one collides with the backing services as they start.
TINY_LEDGER_SUBNET=10.89.0.0/24
TINY_LEDGER_TRAEFIK_IP=10.89.0.250

# The application's forwarded-header trust, and the one variable in this file that is a SECURITY
# CONTROL rather than a convenience. `docker-compose.yml` sets it from TINY_LEDGER_TRAEFIK_IP for
# you; it is named here because a real deployment must set it to its own ingress address.
#
# Widening it — to a subnet, or to Boot's default, which covers the whole 172.16.0.0/12 range Docker
# hands to Compose networks — lets any caller that can reach the application forge a client address
# and walk past §6.1's per-IP backstop. `ForwardedHeaderSpoofingTest` goes red if that happens.
LEDGER_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.89.0.250

# ── Grafana Cloud (§14 step 9 parts 2 and 3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kept in its own `.env.grafana` in practice, like SONAR_TOKEN above, so it can be sourced alone.
# The names live here; the values never do.
#
# `.mcp.json` reads the two below by `${VAR}` expansion, so the MCP server definition is committed
# and the credential is not. **Expansion reads the environment Claude Code was LAUNCHED with** —
# nothing loads a dotenv file, here or in the application (see AGENTS.md, "Configuration"). Source
# the file, then start the agent:
#
#     set -a; . ./.env.grafana; set +a && claude
#
# GRAFANA_URL has a default in `.mcp.json` (the project's stack, not a secret), so only the token is
# strictly required. The token is a **Viewer** service-account token: read-only is enforced by the
# role server-side, which a client flag could not guarantee.
GRAFANA_URL=
GRAFANA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=

# OTLP export — the write path, used by the opt-in Collector rather than by the application
# directly. The endpoint is not secret; the headers value is, because it embeds the access-policy
# token as Basic base64(instanceID:token). Access policy scopes: metrics:write, logs:write,
# traces:write. **CI does not get these** — §14 step 9's gate is a Collector receiving OTLP, which
# a debug exporter satisfies with no secret at all, so a fork's build still passes.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
# The Collector takes a SINGLE Authorization header value, not the SDK's comma-separated
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS form — `docker/otel-collector.yaml` names this variable directly, and the
# two files have to agree or the container starts and silently fails every export. Renamed from
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS at §14 step 9 part 3, when the Collector that reads it was written.
#
# QUOTE IT. The value contains a space ("Basic <base64>"), and `set -a; . ./.env.grafana` on an
# unquoted value stops at that space — the Collector then authenticates with the literal word `Basic`
# and Grafana answers 401, which reads exactly like a bad token. Measured, having cost a debugging
# round: the variable came back 5 characters long.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS_AUTHORIZATION="Basic <base64 of instanceID:token>"
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
GRAFANA_CLOUD_INSTANCE_ID=
GRAFANA_CLOUD_OTLP_TOKEN=
